    "q-90966": {
      "dom_id": "step-q90966",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Given the equation ${y}^{″}-16{y}^{′}+64y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?",
      "worked_solution": "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-16\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+64{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-16\\lambda +64) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-16\\lambda +64 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\lambda -8)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =8$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{8x}$ and $x{e}^{8x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${x}^{2}{e}^{8x}$ is not a solution.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
